# Checkout-byte determinism for governed artifacts.
#
# Governed artifacts are verified by SHA-256 over their bytes — the AAC
# divisions CSV against a digest registered in source, the canonical master
# Markdown and every mounted workbook against the run manifest. Git for Windows
# ships core.autocrlf=true at system scope, so an independent clone that has
# never had a local override checks those text artifacts out as CRLF and every
# registered digest fails. That is a checkout property, not a repository
# property, so it cannot be fixed by a local config: it is pinned here, where
# the repository itself carries the answer.
#
# Recorded so the intent is not re-derived later: at the commit that introduced
# this file every tracked text blob was already pure LF and the only binaries
# were the eight .xlsx workbooks. These rules therefore normalize nothing —
# they hold the bytes that are already committed.

# Fail closed. Without an explicit rule Git applies text=auto's content
# heuristic, which makes checkout bytes depend on what a blob happens to
# contain. Unknown and future file types are byte-preserved instead of guessed;
# every type actually present is named below and overrides this line.
*                  -text

# --- governed text: byte identity is load-bearing ---------------------------
# CSV carries a digest registered in aac_divisions.py; Markdown and JSON are
# digested into the input manifest and the reproducibility artifacts.
*.csv              text eol=lf
*.md               text eol=lf
*.json             text eol=lf

# --- source and configuration text ------------------------------------------
# No registered digest today. Pinned anyway so a future digest over source or
# config cannot be broken by the checkout that produced it.
*.py               text eol=lf
*.toml             text eol=lf
*.ini              text eol=lf
*.sql              text eol=lf
*.mako             text eol=lf
*.yml              text eol=lf
*.yaml             text eol=lf
.gitignore         text eol=lf
.gitattributes     text eol=lf

# --- Windows-native scripts --------------------------------------------------
# Determinism is the requirement, not LF specifically. These are launched by
# Windows PowerShell, so they are pinned to the platform-native ending — pinned
# explicitly, which is what stops autocrlf from deciding it per clone.
*.ps1              text eol=crlf

# --- binary: never touched ---------------------------------------------------
# The Board of Record and every governed workbook. `binary` unsets text, diff
# and merge, so no filter can alter a byte.
*.xlsx             binary

# Raw source captures for the historical bridge lane (2020, 2025). The `* -text`
# rule above already byte-preserves them; the type is named because this file's
# convention is that every type actually present is named, and because raw
# custody depends on the rule rather than merely benefiting from it. Each
# capture is a gzip container whose decompressed bytes carry a registered
# SHA-256, so a checkout filter that touched one would break every digest
# recorded against it. It is also why the captures are stored as `.json.gz` and
# not `.json`: the `*.json` rule above normalises to LF, and a raw capture a
# checkout can rewrite is not a raw capture.
*.gz               binary
